ALVA — Implement Character #0006: A True 3-in-1 Synchronized Fighter

Task

Inspect the entire Alva repository and implement a new character, #0006, using the sprite sheet attached to this conversation.

Character #0006 is fundamentally different from the existing roster. It is THREE characters functioning together as ONE playable fighter.

This is not a character that summons two companions. It is not a character that switches between three forms. It is not three independently controlled fighters.

The player controls all three simultaneously, using exactly one controller, one movement system, one shared combat state, one Launch Point, and one shared defensive hitbox.

All three must be visibly present in the arena together throughout gameplay.

This request focuses on defining and implementing the synchronized three-in-one character system. Do not invent, enumerate, or describe individual attacks. Their specific designs can be established separately.

⸻

1. The fundamental concept: THREE bodies, ONE fighter

Think of #0006 as a single fighter whose visual body consists of three individual characters.

The three individuals must each have their own appearance, animation frames, relative position, and capacity to participate in attacks.

However, gameplay must recognize them collectively as exactly one fighter.

The correct conceptual structure is:

* ONE selectable character: #0006.
* ONE player or CPU controller.
* ONE logical Fighter instance.
* ONE shared position and movement state.
* ONE shared Launch Point.
* ONE defensive hurtbox and collision body.
* ONE shared Energy system.
* ONE shared combat state, including stun, cooldowns, attack commitment, and hitstop.
* THREE independently rendered sprites that belong to that same fighter.
* THREE coordinated animation tracks controlled by the same gameplay state.

Do not create three separate Fighter instances.

Do not give each individual their own health, Launch Point, physics body, collision box, or autonomous decision-making.

The player must never need to manually select which individual they are controlling.

All three are always controlled as one unit.

2. Permanent presence

All three individuals must remain present together for the entire duration that #0006 is in play.

This is non-negotiable.

When #0006 spawns, all three appear.

When #0006 stands idle, all three remain visible.

When #0006 moves, all three move.

When #0006 attacks, all three remain present, even when only one performs the attack.

When #0006 gets hurt, all three react.

When #0006 is launched, all three are launched together.

When #0006 respawns, all three return together.

An individual must never disappear simply because another individual is performing an action.

No character switching, temporary despawning, replacing one sprite with another, or summon behavior.

The three should have recognizable spacing, preserving the impression that they are three distinct individuals standing together.

They can change their relative positions and animation poses when necessary, but must remain part of one cohesive formation.

3. Synchronized movement

Every universal movement action applies to all three at the same time.

This includes:

* Idle and turning.
* Running and stopping.
* Jumping, including subsequent air jumps.
* Falling and fast-falling.
* Landing.
* Ground Dash.
* Air Dash.
* Hurt and aerial hurt.
* Hitstun and knockback.
* Ground shielding.
* Shield preparation and release.
* Recovery from launches.
* Respawning and elimination.

When the player presses Jump, all three jump simultaneously.

When the player runs right, all three run right.

When the player changes direction, all three turn with the formation.

When the player Dashes, all three Dash in synchronization.

When the fighter falls off a platform, all three fall together.

They must not jump independently, follow each other with delayed physics, or require different movement inputs.

Their animations can be different because they have different sprite artwork, but their underlying movement states and state transitions must be synchronized.

For example, during a jump, all three can display their own jump animations while sharing the exact same jump velocity, gravity, and landing event.

Do not implement the three individuals as a leader and two followers that must catch up.

Instead, implement one shared physics body with three visual representations positioned relative to it.

The three sprites can have configurable local offsets and formation animations, but those offsets must not create separate gameplay movement.

Respect Alva’s existing universal movement rules. Character #0006 must not override the baseline running, jumping, Dash, or air-dash physics.

4. Formation and positioning

The three individuals must appear as a coordinated group.

Their positions should be derived from the single fighter’s world position, using relative formation offsets.

The formation must support:

* A natural default idle arrangement.
* Maintaining recognizable separation while running.
* Synchronized airborne positioning.
* Formation adjustments for attacks.
* Members stepping backward while another attacks.
* Smooth returns to the normal formation.
* Correct horizontal mirroring when changing facing direction.

They must not overlap permanently into what looks like a single sprite.

They must not spread apart into completely independent entities.

Use a stable formation anchor so the trio’s movement remains understandable to the player.

Relative positioning is visual coordination, not separate physics.

Avoid abrupt teleportation between formation arrangements. Position changes should be visually smooth and deterministic.

5. Attack coordination — critically important

Although they collectively function as one fighter, all three individuals are capable of performing attacks.

However, this does NOT mean they all automatically attack whenever the player presses an attack button.

Standard single-member attacks

For an attack assigned to one individual:

1. The designated individual performs the attack animation.
2. The other two do not perform that attack.
3. The other two move backward relative to the direction the formation is facing, creating space for the attacking individual.
4. Both non-attacking individuals remain visible and synchronized with the group.
5. After the attack, the three smoothly return to their normal arrangement.

The non-attacking members are not idle summons, and they are not acting independently. Their backward movement is a coordinated part of the group’s animation.

Two-member combination attacks

The system must also support an attack that intentionally involves TWO members.

In this case:

* Both designated members participate in the attack.
* The third member moves backward and remains present.
* The two participants coordinate their action as one logical attack.
* The entire trio remains controlled by the same player and shared combat state.

Three-member combination attacks

The system must also support an attack involving ALL THREE members.

In this case:

* All three participate in the same coordinated action.
* Each individual can have its own animation sequence.
* All three sequences belong to one logical move.
* The move follows Alva’s normal combat rules.

Essential distinction

One, two, or three members may participate in a move, but every move still belongs to one fighter.

Do not implement attacks as three independently triggered combat actions.

The system should support specifying which members participate in any given attack using reusable, declarative character data.

Single-member attacks are the normal behavior. Two-member and three-member participation must occur only when explicitly defined.

No automatic simultaneous attacking by all three.

No independent attack cooldowns for individual members.

No autonomous attacks by non-participating members.

No extra damage simply because multiple sprites are visible.

A combination attack is one coordinated move, with whatever strike events are explicitly authored for that move.

Do not design the individual moves in this task. Implement the participation and coordination system so that later attack definitions can use it.

6. One Launch Point, one hurtbox, one physics body

This is the most important gameplay requirement after synchronization.

All three share exactly ONE Launch Point.

If an opponent lands a hit on #0006, the damage is applied to the shared fighter, not to a specific individual.

All three react to that hit together.

There must be:

* No separate Launch Point for each individual.
* No individual damage accumulation.
* No individual deaths or knockouts.
* No separate hitstun timers.
* No independent knockback velocities.
* No ability for the opponent to eliminate only one member.

Use ONE shared defensive hurtbox for the entire trio, positioned around the group’s logical body.

The opponent is fighting character #0006, not three targets.

The formation’s extra visible bodies must not create additional independent vulnerable collision regions.

Hitting the shared hurtbox triggers one normal hit event, with one application of damage, Launch Point, hitstun, and launch physics.

Do not triple damage because three characters are shown.

Do not let one area attack hit all three separately.

Do not create three pushboxes or three platform-collision bodies.

The shared hitbox requirement concerns the trio’s defensive collision and vulnerability. Individual moves may still have their normal offensive hitboxes, following Alva’s existing attack definitions.

When a member performs a move, its offensive effects must line up with its visible animation and position, without creating an additional vulnerable Fighter entity.

Keep the game’s combat rules consistent.

7. Shared hurt and launch synchronization

When the shared fighter takes a hit:

All three must respond to that same hit.

They should enter the appropriate hurt animation on the same simulation step.

If the hit launches the fighter, all three travel together under one velocity and one launch calculation.

They may use different hurt poses, but they must never become separated into three independently launched bodies.

The same applies to:

* Ground hitstun.
* Aerial hitstun.
* Tumbling.
* Hitstop.
* Launch rebounds.
* Being pushed or pulled.
* Recovery.
* Falling into the Void.
* Elimination.
* Respawning.

Any gameplay event that normally affects an entire fighter must affect the complete trio.

8. Shield synchronization

Ground shielding is a synchronized defensive action.

When the player activates the Shield, all three must visibly react to the shielding state.

All three should adopt appropriate defensive or guarding animations.

They share one Shield state, one protection calculation, one Energy bar, and one hit/block resolution.

Do not give them three independent Shields.

Do not make the opponent break through the Shield by attacking a different visual member.

When Shield is released, all three return to their normal shared state.

Use Alva’s existing Shield mechanics, including its existing Energy rules, perfect-block behavior, and restrictions.

9. Deflect is different

Deflect must be performed by only ONE designated individual at a time.

This is an intentional exception to the shared-action animation behavior.

When the player uses the aerial Deflect input:

* One designated member performs the Deflect.
* The other two remain visible.
* The other two can assume appropriate coordinated non-attacking poses or shift backward.
* The Deflect uses ONE logical combat action.
* Only the designated participant visually executes the Deflect.
* Its hitbox and projectile interaction use Alva’s existing Deflect rules.

Do not automatically make all three Deflect simultaneously.

Do not create three overlapping Deflect hitboxes.

Do not spend Energy three times.

The Deflect still belongs to the complete #0006 fighter, even though only one member visibly performs it.

