Final clarification — what 3-in-1 actually means

The most important thing to understand is the difference between three characters controlled together and three separate characters following one controller.

I want the first.

There must be three distinct individuals visually present and capable of participating in combat, but the underlying game must fundamentally understand them as one character.

One input moves all three.

One Jump makes all three jump.

One Dash makes all three Dash.

One hit hurts all three.

One launch sends all three flying.

One Shield protects the shared fighter.

One Deflect makes one designated member perform the Deflect.

One regular attack involves one member while the others step back.

One specifically defined combination attack may involve two or three members.

One Launch Point tracks the entire trio.

One defensive hitbox represents the entire trio.

THREE visible characters. ONE playable fighter. ONE unified gameplay entity.

Do not solve this by implementing companions, followers, AI teammates, summons, character switching, or three overlapping Fighter instances.

The goal is for #0006 to genuinely feel like the player is controlling three synchronized characters as one unified fighting character.

Prioritize getting this fundamental architecture correct before implementing or proposing any specific attack designs.

